WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:21.200
Hi, this is Robert Scheer with another edition of Sheer Intelligence, where the, you know,

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:26.860
it's funny, I got the name of the show, I said, it's sort of the poor man's central

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:33.860
intelligence agency. So I was using Sheer Intelligence to see my own little check. And in fact, I began

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:39.980
journalistic life at Grandparts Magazine before I went to work in the establishment LA Times,

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:48.220
doing stories criticizing the CIA, criticizing the intelligence agencies. And today, we're

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:54.660
going to have a show with two people who spent the better part of their lives, productive lives

00:00:55.100 --> 00:01:03.780
at the CIA. One is Ray McGovern. And he joined, well, you know, just about when I went to work for

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:09.620
Grandparts, you went to work for the CIA. So we were on the opposite side. And you stayed there

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:18.060
until 1990, that's 27 years with the CIA. And you were not a whistleblower. You, in fact,

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:25.020
worked on the, under the Bush administration, you were the CIA person doing preparing the

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:33.260
national intelligence estimates. And you prepared the president's daily brief. And you received the

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:41.820
intelligence commendation medal when you retired, which you returned in 206 to protest the CIA's

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:50.380
involvement in torture, which brings up our other guests, John Kiriakou, who exposed that torture

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:57.580
program, refused to participate in, was actually involved in the capture of the highest, then

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:05.340
the highest ranking alleged al-Qaeda suspect, the man who was still in Guantanamo and in prison,

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:13.020
but has not had a day in court. And you refuse to be trained in torture. You didn't believe in it.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:18.700
And of course, as we know from the US Senate, torture report, the part that's been released,

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:25.660
the basic report has not been released, but in a heavily redacted version of the introduction,

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:31.740
we find that torture never produced any actionable intelligence. But nonetheless,

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:39.340
here you have Ray McGovern, who gave back his medal, his intelligence commendation medal,

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:44.780
in response to, really, to the revelations that came from you. And what I want to do,

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:51.500
you are two guys who, you know, try to serve your country, did so, were honored for that.

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:58.940
So were you, John, and promoted after the capture of what was supposed to be the highest

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:10.300
known captured al-Qaeda member. And what happened was that you have run into a really,

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:15.340
I don't know, I think of Lillian Hellman, when she wrote about the McCarthy period she wrote,

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:22.380
had a book called The Scoundrel Time. And, you know, really, you're in a period now

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:29.500
where you guys are in danger of being considered fake news, making up news, you're, you know,

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:34.620
you're going to be made non-person. If I, in fact, just doing this discussion with you,

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:39.820
and I've interviewed you both before, you know, the whole program could be dismissed

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:46.940
as fake news. And we haven't lived in a time like this, where suddenly people who really know a great

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:54.860
deal, and are speaking honestly, and John, in Kariaka's case, he served two years in jail

00:03:54.860 --> 00:04:00.620
for having revealed aspects of the torture program. But the fact of the matter is,

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:07.180
you are being made into non-persons. And in fact, you now can only get a form if you go on

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:12.860
something, well, we don't have RT anymore. We've had Sputnik, or you go on some smaller

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:21.420
shows, anti-war, libertarian website, some few left on the left and so forth. So let me just

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:30.780
bring that up. How did two highly regarded CIA people get to be so disreputable in the eyes

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:43.340
of mass media? John, I think you're more disreputable than I. But you've been arrested more times than I have.

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:50.940
It'll help if you guys identify, because your voice is, even though I know you don't,

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:56.220
I know Ray comes from the Bronx, where I'm from. John, you come from around, I guess,

00:04:56.220 --> 00:05:01.020
Virginia, right? No, from Pittsburgh. Oh, Pittsburgh, okay. Well, that's close enough.

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:08.700
When you speak, and you both know each other, and I forget the name of the organization,

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:18.620
Veteran Intelligence Professionals for Sanity. So anyway, use your first name when you speak,

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:27.980
okay? Sure. So I'm John Kiriakou. John, I'll use my first name. Yeah, you know, I think Ray would

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:35.660
probably agree with me that this war between Russia and Ukraine has put many of us in an

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:43.020
awkward position. Not just many of us, formerly in the intelligence services, but many of us

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:50.860
on the political left. Because if you say anything right now that's not directly in the mainstream,

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:57.500
you're dismissed. You're dismissed as an apologist for the Russians, as pro-Putin,

00:05:58.060 --> 00:06:03.580
as someone who hates America, or doesn't know what he's talking about.

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:09.820
You really can't have an alternative view. And if you do, if you publish that view,

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:17.580
you're easily banned from Facebook, from Twitter, from any number of different platforms.

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:25.100
It says, though, that if you have anything to do now with the media, including the progressive

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:33.420
media, you have to toe the line on this war or you're silenced. Well, that's a pretty ominous

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:40.700
situation to be in. You know, as I say, as someone who spoke out and wrote critically about

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:47.660
the Vietnam War and continued to do that sort of thing. I personally have never been in a period

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:54.300
as intimidating as this one. And the use of this notion of disinformation or fake news

00:06:55.180 --> 00:07:01.900
to totally disparage anyone who may disagree with you. We don't even seem to have any

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:09.660
visible peace movement cautioning about sending in all these arms into Ukraine, whatever you think

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:19.260
about it. I gather from you, John, you condemn the Russian invasion. Well, in fact, despite the

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:25.580
fact that I work for the Russians, I have a daily radio show for two hours on Sputnik Radio,

00:07:25.580 --> 00:07:30.780
which is a Russian government-funded radio network. I said on the very first day of the

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:39.020
invasion that I unreservedly condemned the Russian invasion of Ukraine because I believe that all

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:45.260
wars are wrong, they're violations of international law. That's not to say that I don't understand the

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:52.220
Russian reasoning for crossing the border. I understand that the Russians objected to the

00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:59.180
US-sponsored coup in 2014, that the Russians believed that ethnic Russians in Ukraine were

00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:10.220
being discriminated against, et cetera, et cetera. But I'm still viewed as a Russian apologist,

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:16.460
even though I've made clear my personal position. It's funny, somebody said to me the other day,

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:21.100
well, if you were really against the war, you would resign. And I said, yeah, that's easy

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:25.580
for you to say, but are you going to pay my bills? Are you going to put my kids through

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:33.420
college? Well, you should mention, by the way, that this is not a minor matter with you, because

00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:40.540
you lost your pension speaking honestly about torture. We talk about war crimes in Ukraine.

00:08:40.540 --> 00:08:46.780
I mean, clearly the US policy of systematic torture in black sites all over the world

00:08:46.780 --> 00:08:53.980
is one of the great crimes of modern history. And you suffered, you lost your CIA pension.

00:08:54.860 --> 00:09:01.420
You know, your inability, you can't get on these other shows. And you are, and in fact,

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:08.460
your work as Sputnik is kind of a Potemkin radio program, meaning it doesn't really exist,

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:14.380
since no one in this country, I mean, hardly anyone in this country can hear Sputnik.

00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:20.780
That's a good point. That's a good point, Bob, that I actually wouldn't have thought to bring

00:09:20.780 --> 00:09:27.180
up myself. Before the war, you could listen to my show pretty much anywhere. We were on YouTube

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:36.860
every day, we were on Spreaker, Spotify, iTunes, iHeartRadio, tune in, and we had 38, well,

00:09:36.860 --> 00:09:45.020
35 to 38,000 downloads a day. So that's a pretty healthy daily audience, 35 to 38,000.

00:09:45.020 --> 00:09:51.180
We've been stricken from every one of those platforms. And now the only way you can listen

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:59.740
to our show is on the Sputnik news website, or on a 1000 watt terrestrial

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:07.000
radio station in Washington D.C. that you can barely pick up if you just, you know, cross

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:14.300
the street. And so we're down to about 500 listeners a day. So you're right. It's a Potemkin

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:20.280
village kind of radio station in that almost nobody listens to it.

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:26.440
Yeah. And that's not totally a matter of choice. They can't get the signal. So I want

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:33.200
to bring in Ray McGovern, because as opposed to John, at least you kept your pension and

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:39.520
you were honored. And you, you know, you actually work with Henry Kissinger when Nixon was president

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:46.300
or John did also work with, you work with John Kerry, a senator, and you worked on the

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:52.840
Hill and so forth. But Ray McGovern, you became, you know, an advocate for peace. Let's

00:10:52.840 --> 00:11:01.160
face it. And you now are, you know, I've interviewed people for this show who don't talk to you,

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:07.640
who are in the CIA and have written you threatening emails and so forth and whatever happened

00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:13.580
to Ray McGovern. So tell us, how did you become such a dangerous figure? You seem

00:11:13.580 --> 00:11:21.460
like a nice old guy. You know, what happened here? You take it a lot of gold, you take it,

00:11:21.460 --> 00:11:27.900
you take it a lot of gold from from Putin. Is that what's going on? What's happening?

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:36.540
I wish. No, you know, mention was made a little earlier to scoundrel time. And when

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:44.580
I entered the CIA, there were scoundrels around, but most of them existed in the operations

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:51.660
part where they tried to assassinate Castro and did assassinate some other folks, where

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:57.980
we kept hermetically sealed away from those guys. But as time went by and Bill Casey

00:11:57.980 --> 00:12:05.260
came in under Reagan as CIA director, and as he chose Bobby Gates, who said all the

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:10.680
right things about the Russians and how the Russians would never give up power in the Communist

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:16.700
Party, they recruited people in managerial positions who were scoundrels, who wanted

00:12:16.700 --> 00:12:23.260
to get promoted. And they were they were in power at the first experience that I had

00:12:23.260 --> 00:12:30.140
that I lived through. And it wasn't Afghanistan, it wasn't Ukraine, it was Iraq. And that

00:12:30.140 --> 00:12:37.900
turned me because I saw my former colleagues deliberately falsifying, making up, manufacturing

00:12:37.900 --> 00:12:44.980
evidence to justify a war. Now it doesn't get any worse than that, okay, not not only from

00:12:44.980 --> 00:12:50.900
a professional point of view, but from a from an ethical point of view. So that was the

00:12:50.900 --> 00:12:56.860
first time. Now, were we given any any play in the media? Well, I'll tell you, when

00:12:56.940 --> 00:13:05.300
veteran intelligence professionals for sanity was founded, and that was January 2003, we

00:13:05.300 --> 00:13:10.500
wrote a memo to the president, President Bush, we chose to assume that he didn't know what's

00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:17.300
going on. But we told him the same day as Colin Powell spoke, that there was dribble

00:13:17.340 --> 00:13:22.900
that was all made up. And that if he didn't widen the circle of his advisors beyond

00:13:22.900 --> 00:13:29.820
those clearly bent on a war, it would be a catastrophe. Now, we take no pleasure in the

00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:35.140
fact that we're right on that. But that was the monstrous thing of the first, quote, war

00:13:35.140 --> 00:13:42.340
of choice. And it was, quote, justified, end quote, by evidence that later, the Senate

00:13:42.380 --> 00:13:48.420
Committee said was made up was uncorrelated and was what was the last word?

00:13:49.260 --> 00:13:57.140
And who was it? It was made up. That was the word. So, so that was the first. Now, we

00:13:57.140 --> 00:14:02.660
were given much play after our initial thing. And our initial thing went out on the AFP

00:14:02.660 --> 00:14:08.140
wire. And that's why we got some play. Since then, we've been pretty much shunned, not

00:14:08.140 --> 00:14:14.740
only on that, but on Afghanistan. We wrote a memo to President Obama saying, Welcome

00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:23.140
to Vietnam, Mr. Obama, you're letting these generals and these sycophants like Bobby Gates

00:14:24.020 --> 00:14:29.100
run roughshod over you, and you're making some really bad decisions on Afghanistan. We

00:14:29.100 --> 00:14:34.140
told him that he didn't, he didn't understand. When Hillary Clinton came to

00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:38.220
George Washington University and I wangled the ticket from my friend, Larry

00:14:38.220 --> 00:14:43.980
Wilkerson, I simply stood up and turned my back to her, because I couldn't let

00:14:43.980 --> 00:14:47.860
everyone there think that she was the darling that was going to be president and

00:14:47.860 --> 00:14:53.460
she was the cat's pajamas, as we used to say on the Bronx. So, you know, it became

00:14:53.460 --> 00:14:58.380
pretty, pretty corrupt. When you talk about scoundrel time in Washington, it

00:14:58.380 --> 00:15:03.380
began under Bobby Gates and Bill Casey, but progressively these people projected

00:15:03.380 --> 00:15:09.980
or promoted into positions of influence and power were worse and worse. And

00:15:09.980 --> 00:15:14.300
that's why we have now, on Ukraine, a situation where I don't know who's in

00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:18.620
charge. Biden is certainly not in charge. What kind of, what kind of

00:15:18.620 --> 00:15:21.980
information he's getting from his CIA director or from the National

00:15:21.980 --> 00:15:25.660
Intelligence Director? I don't know. They seem a little bit more sane than

00:15:25.660 --> 00:15:28.500
the rest of them, but that's not saying, that's damning with pain and

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:36.620
praise. Time for a break. We'll be back in a few minutes. We're back with

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:43.340
your intelligence and our guests. Well, you know, I do want to get to a point here

00:15:43.340 --> 00:15:50.740
when you guys joined the CIA and, you know, when you were working with Kissinger,

00:15:50.740 --> 00:15:58.220
you know, Kissinger, you were still in the CIA then, Kissinger and Nixon went

00:15:58.220 --> 00:16:06.260
to China and they negotiated peace and the end of really the negative

00:16:06.260 --> 00:16:11.660
relations with China and mission of China back into the UN and diplomatic

00:16:11.660 --> 00:16:16.900
relations. And China is still a communist country, so is Vietnam. But we

00:16:16.900 --> 00:16:20.340
get along quite well with them, particularly we like Vietnam even more

00:16:20.700 --> 00:16:26.780
than China. And no one ever said that Kissinger was a Mao

00:16:26.980 --> 00:16:32.900
Zedong agent. They were getting along with the most ruthless communist

00:16:32.900 --> 00:16:37.900
leaders. The same thing was true of negotiation of arms control with

00:16:37.900 --> 00:16:43.420
Brezhnev, with Khrushchev, with the Soviet communist leadership. The irony

00:16:43.420 --> 00:16:48.300
in this situation is that we have red baiting now with Russia and

00:16:48.300 --> 00:16:54.020
Putin without a red. And we've got an image of Putin. I mean, because

00:16:54.020 --> 00:16:58.300
when we said a red, there were communist parties and they had some

00:16:58.300 --> 00:17:02.300
ideology and they had some clarity about what they were doing. And there was a

00:17:02.300 --> 00:17:06.660
lot of argument about whether you could ever negotiate with these folks. And

00:17:06.820 --> 00:17:11.220
some of our most ruthless Cold Warriors, Richard Nixon, of course, right

00:17:11.220 --> 00:17:17.860
there, negotiated peace with them. Now we're in a position where to

00:17:17.860 --> 00:17:23.980
say we shouldn't be negotiating with the Ukrainians, with Putin,

00:17:24.260 --> 00:17:29.300
and so forth. That's heresy now. There is no peace movement. There was a

00:17:29.300 --> 00:17:33.100
peace movement saying we could have arms control and get along and

00:17:33.100 --> 00:17:37.660
negotiate with Mao Zedong, you know, even with Joseph Stalin at one

00:17:37.660 --> 00:17:44.100
point. But now to suggest that maybe just throwing arms in and

00:17:44.100 --> 00:17:49.660
risking nuclear war, you advocate that. And my God, you're the worst

00:17:49.660 --> 00:17:55.100
kind of traitor. How did we get to this point? And it really goes to

00:17:55.100 --> 00:18:01.180
this word intelligence. We did figure out in the worst days of the CIA

00:18:01.420 --> 00:18:06.140
that you could actually negotiate with these folks that there might be a

00:18:06.140 --> 00:18:10.420
common stake in arms control and limiting war and so forth. That's

00:18:10.420 --> 00:18:16.300
gone out the window now. And the image now of Putin and Russia, and

00:18:16.300 --> 00:18:20.500
this is they have no validity in any of their demands of any of their

00:18:20.500 --> 00:18:27.100
concerns. And they just are evil incarnate. And that is, I think I

00:18:27.100 --> 00:18:29.620
would suggest a very dangerous moment.

00:18:31.060 --> 00:18:34.420
Well, you know, let me let me comment on that because my proudest

00:18:34.420 --> 00:18:38.900
moment working in the analysis. This is Ray. Yeah, go ahead.

00:18:39.260 --> 00:18:43.580
Yeah, I'm sorry. This is Ray McGovern. I was chief for the

00:18:43.580 --> 00:18:48.620
Soviet foreign policy branch back in the 70s. My proudest moment was

00:18:49.540 --> 00:18:54.980
proving with my analysts, of course, that the Soviets and the

00:18:54.980 --> 00:18:58.500
Chinese where they chose throats, they were likely to come to

00:18:58.500 --> 00:19:03.540
blows, which they did in 1969. And that you could you could if

00:19:03.540 --> 00:19:06.060
you were Kissinger and Nixon, if you were smart enough, you

00:19:06.060 --> 00:19:10.220
could exploit this hostility. That's what they chose to do.

00:19:10.700 --> 00:19:15.340
One of my nice memorabilia is a nice note I got back from Kissinger

00:19:15.340 --> 00:19:18.500
saying, you got it right, we're going to do this. They went to

00:19:18.500 --> 00:19:23.020
China. And there were really impediments to movement toward

00:19:23.060 --> 00:19:28.140
strategic arms negotiations with with Moscow, they disappeared.

00:19:28.340 --> 00:19:33.460
And before you know it, we had an agreement in Moscow on May in

00:19:33.460 --> 00:19:40.220
May, 1972, called the anti ballistic missile treaty, which

00:19:40.580 --> 00:19:45.780
formed the the form the foundation of strategic stability

00:19:45.780 --> 00:19:49.980
for the next 30 years count them three decades until the

00:19:49.980 --> 00:19:54.140
junior Bush get out of there. So our input, you know, our

00:19:54.140 --> 00:19:57.500
analysis that look, you could take advantage of this was key.

00:19:57.500 --> 00:20:00.020
And then and this was one of the things we could do.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:06.900
and were able to back in the day. How about now? Well, I find myself in the same position

00:20:06.900 --> 00:20:14.840
looking at Russian-Chinese relations, and I see that there's been another tectonic shift.

00:20:14.840 --> 00:20:20.760
Tectonic I use advisedly. China and Russia are together now. I don't think Putin would

00:20:20.760 --> 00:20:25.940
have done, would have risked doing what he did in Ukraine, were he not to have full

00:20:25.940 --> 00:20:32.020
backing from Xi Jinping, the president of China. That changes the world, folks. Putin

00:20:32.020 --> 00:20:38.820
is not going to lose in Ukraine, and NATO is going to keep stoking the fires until we all

00:20:38.820 --> 00:20:45.340
wear out, until we're all dead. So this is really important, because when we talk about

00:20:45.340 --> 00:20:51.360
China, and when we talk about Ukraine, all these things are interrelated, and the

00:20:51.360 --> 00:20:55.960
numbskulls that are heading up the State Department and the National Security Council

00:20:55.960 --> 00:21:00.520
don't seem to have it in their heads that China and Russia are together, as a matter

00:21:00.520 --> 00:21:07.520
of fact, and this last thing I'll say on this, on June 16th of last year, when Biden met

