with Putin and Geneva, the only thing we know about how that went is when Biden's trying

00:21:13.040 --> 00:21:17.280
to get on the plane, they say, come on, Joe, get on. Joe, no, no, no, I want to say

00:21:17.280 --> 00:21:21.600
something more than a press. Okay, what do you want to say? Well, I told, I, this is

00:21:21.600 --> 00:21:26.720
not appropriate to say exactly what I told Putin, but I, you know, he's got a real problem.

00:21:26.720 --> 00:21:33.400
China is squeezing Russia. China has a long border. They're aspiring not only to be the

00:21:33.400 --> 00:21:40.720
biggest economic power, but also to be, oh, Putin has a real problem with China. Now,

00:21:40.720 --> 00:21:45.920
I don't know who told Biden to tell Putin that, but I would have loved a bit of fly

00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:51.600
on the wall when Putin turned around to his advisors and said, huh? Yeah, and whatever,

00:21:51.600 --> 00:21:57.440
the tensions between China and Russia, and this is really something the mass media seems to have

00:21:57.440 --> 00:22:06.080
missed. I don't quite get it, but NATO is now an alliance in a new Cold War that will and could

00:22:06.080 --> 00:22:12.480
extend certainly to China, but maybe to India. We don't know. I mean, the whole idea that

00:22:12.480 --> 00:22:19.200
there's a zone of security basically built on Europe with the, you know, U.S. really calling the

00:22:19.200 --> 00:22:27.920
shots is, you know, it represents the development of a unipolar world again. I mean, you know,

00:22:28.480 --> 00:22:33.040
which we thought we had after the end of the Cold War. And what are you basically saying?

00:22:33.040 --> 00:22:38.080
You're saying to these other folks, if you want to have security, you better tighten

00:22:38.080 --> 00:22:45.760
some other alliances. What I'm saying, Bob, is not a unipolar. That was what we had after World War

00:22:45.760 --> 00:22:52.000
II and pretty much if the Soviet Union fell apart. Right now it's a bipolar world, okay? A bipolar

00:22:52.000 --> 00:22:57.360
in the psychiatric sense as well as in the political sense. No, really, we have the

00:22:57.920 --> 00:23:08.000
the, the, the lily quite West against people of color who happen to represent the majority of

00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:13.440
people in this world. Okay, we have China, we have India, we have South Africa, we have got

00:23:13.440 --> 00:23:19.440
Brazil, and we've got, well, we're black in the Russians so much that they're people of color

00:23:19.440 --> 00:23:25.440
now. And there are more of them than there are of us. And it's a no win situation for

00:23:25.440 --> 00:23:31.680
us lily white people from the West to think that we can predominate that we think there can be a

00:23:31.680 --> 00:23:38.320
unipolar world again. It's bipolar and the, the cards are stacked against us as I've just tried to

00:23:38.320 --> 00:23:44.320
explain. You know, it's interesting you mentioned that, you know, about how we think of Russia

00:23:44.320 --> 00:23:52.480
and obviously, you know, is a white, basically white population. However, the anti-Slavic

00:23:53.200 --> 00:23:57.840
current that, you know, after all Hitler played on the anti-Slavic as well.

00:23:59.040 --> 00:24:07.280
And along, along with anti-Semitism was a lesser people. And, you know, that doesn't sit well,

00:24:07.280 --> 00:24:12.560
but that keeps coming up. Somehow the Russian troops, you know, whatever you think about,

00:24:12.560 --> 00:24:18.800
you know, obviously, I personally am very opposed to any military invasion. I don't believe

00:24:18.880 --> 00:24:24.960
in war, I believe in peace. But nonetheless, you know, Russian conscripts are now presented

00:24:24.960 --> 00:24:30.800
as savage, whereas Ukrainian troops are enlightened. And there is an echo of that.

00:24:31.680 --> 00:24:39.920
But what I think should be pointed out here is that we are acting as if all the options

00:24:40.640 --> 00:24:46.400
are with NATO. And there's something weird about a military alliance,

00:24:47.040 --> 00:24:54.880
replacing basically the UN as a center of peacekeeping. Is it not the case?

00:24:54.880 --> 00:25:00.400
John, you haven't talked much. Yeah, I think so. Let me just say something before I introduce you,

00:25:00.400 --> 00:25:05.920
John. One of the things that we have is sort of this Orwellian phenomena of the love of having

00:25:05.920 --> 00:25:11.840
an enemy. And you devoted much of your life to this, what was supposed to be the enemy

00:25:11.840 --> 00:25:19.120
that replaced communism, which was a terrorist, right? Muslim terrorism, right? And you were out there

00:25:19.120 --> 00:25:25.920
catching these Muslim terrorists and so forth. Now we've gone back to what? We don't have quite the

00:25:25.920 --> 00:25:33.120
communist, it's just all Russia. And Russia is not that powerful economically that it's going to

00:25:33.120 --> 00:25:37.520
be able to. So you have to then latch on to China or something, but take it from there.

00:25:38.400 --> 00:25:44.560
Yeah, I think you're both raising very important points here. It's something that I saw beginning

00:25:44.560 --> 00:25:52.800
many years ago when I was still at the CIA. And I've been gone now for, wow, 17 years,

00:25:52.800 --> 00:25:59.120
a little bit more than 17 years. But even back in the early 2000s,

00:26:00.160 --> 00:26:06.000
when we had an opportunity to establish really a new kind of relationship with Russia,

00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:12.720
where it would have been actually quite easy to cooperate on a very deep level on issues like

00:26:12.720 --> 00:26:19.040
counterterrorism, counter narcotics, counter proliferation. We both had a terrible time in

00:26:19.040 --> 00:26:27.920
Afghanistan. Afghanistan produces 93% of the world's heroin. And most of that heroin goes to Russia

00:26:27.920 --> 00:26:33.120
and to Iran. We could have really worked together to make the world a better place.

00:26:33.680 --> 00:26:44.480
Not even mentioning the fact that Russia is a Christian country. It wanted at the time to open

00:26:44.480 --> 00:26:51.280
to the West for its own economic development. And we were willing, I guess, to discuss

00:26:52.720 --> 00:27:00.480
these issues on a limited basis, if we could bring the Russians in as a junior partner,

00:27:01.440 --> 00:27:10.240
as a mentee to be westernized. And they valued their independence. They weren't interested.

00:27:10.800 --> 00:27:19.680
We know now, as another example, that both Boris Yeltsin and Vladimir Putin talked to American

00:27:19.680 --> 00:27:25.360
presidents about joining NATO. And they got the cold shoulder. We didn't want them in NATO.

00:27:25.440 --> 00:27:32.480
Well, NATO is really, in my own view, NATO is irrelevant at this point. There was no reason

00:27:32.480 --> 00:27:36.560
not to bring the Russians in. At the very least, it would have been good for peace

00:27:36.560 --> 00:27:41.680
and good for economic development. Your other point, Bob, I think, is right on.

00:27:41.680 --> 00:27:48.960
We always seem to need an ism in this country to rally against, whether it was anarchism

00:27:48.960 --> 00:27:54.640
or socialism or for a very long time communism. It was fascism for a little while during the

00:27:54.640 --> 00:28:02.320
Second World War. Then it was Islamism. And, you know, it's like we just can't seem to function

00:28:02.320 --> 00:28:10.000
unless we have an enemy. Well, I think there's a big issue here. And what remains of our time,

00:28:10.640 --> 00:28:18.960
we should consider. And that's the whole idea of a new, an American Rome, that taking this

00:28:18.960 --> 00:28:27.040
notion of American hegemony, NATO seen in that light becomes a very important instrument of power.

00:28:28.480 --> 00:28:33.840
And the combination of the U.S. and NATO is really saying to the rest of the world,

00:28:33.840 --> 00:28:41.280
our way or the highway. And it's very clear the talk now is going after China. And what is

00:28:41.280 --> 00:28:46.560
really at issue, because we have claimed all along, going back to the founders,

00:28:46.560 --> 00:28:52.080
that we really believed in trade and your economic output and, you know, your ability

00:28:52.800 --> 00:28:58.960
in a notion of capitalism, even when it was a very primitive capitalism. And the big shock of

00:28:58.960 --> 00:29:04.560
modern history is that the most effective capitalist turned out to people who also

00:29:04.560 --> 00:29:10.000
called themselves communists. It's one of the most bizarre twists in human history.

00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:15.840
And it turns out we don't really welcome the Chinese communists as capitalists,

00:29:15.840 --> 00:29:21.280
you know, even though they carried us right through the pandemic and everything else.

00:29:21.840 --> 00:29:29.200
And so you have this weird mixture of a political, militaristic operation like NATO.

00:29:30.320 --> 00:29:36.320
And all this talk about what ideal life, your freedom and democracy you're bringing to the

00:29:36.320 --> 00:29:43.360
world. But your real goal is to hobble another economic system that has the same

00:29:43.360 --> 00:29:50.240
characteristics of a marketplace, China. And the odd thing is you're backing into forming this

00:29:50.240 --> 00:29:57.040
alliance between an underpopulated Russia, rich in resources, an overpopulated China,

00:29:57.040 --> 00:30:00.000
poor in those very same resources, you're creating

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:07.840
creating a real monster of your, in reality, an economic one.

00:30:07.840 --> 00:30:12.200
And that really, to my mind, is what NATO is all about now.

00:30:12.200 --> 00:30:19.520
It's a political, economic, militarist alternative to hold some notion of global power.

00:30:19.520 --> 00:30:20.520
Yeah.

00:30:20.520 --> 00:30:24.800
Well, Bob, this is Ray McGovern again.

00:30:24.800 --> 00:30:31.760
There's a very important strata of society that's profiting by all this, profiteering, I should

00:30:31.760 --> 00:30:32.760
say.

00:30:32.760 --> 00:30:40.200
Cui Bono, well, what Eisenhower warned about 60 years ago, the military industrial complex.

00:30:40.200 --> 00:30:46.120
I mean, Raytheon, if I were to see you or Raytheon or Lockheed, I'm gonna make a money

00:30:46.120 --> 00:30:47.400
hand over fist yet.

00:30:47.400 --> 00:30:53.160
You have to have an enemy, and whether it's an Islam or whether it's a country.

00:30:53.160 --> 00:30:57.560
Now the country of choice happens to be Russia right now.

00:30:57.560 --> 00:31:03.760
It's gonna be China or strategic documents out of the Pentagon say that expressly, that is,

00:31:03.760 --> 00:31:06.200
China is enemy number one.

00:31:06.200 --> 00:31:09.840
Russia doesn't even make it in the top five, you can believe it.

00:31:09.840 --> 00:31:14.280
But we have done Russia, Russia, Russia, Russia gate for five years.

00:31:14.280 --> 00:31:19.320
We've conditioned the American people to believe that Putin is Hitler incarnate.

00:31:19.320 --> 00:31:26.240
And that's what's enabling our current government to play on this fear of Russia.

00:31:26.240 --> 00:31:31.960
To make sure that there's no agreement to stop slaughtering all those Ukrainians that

00:31:31.960 --> 00:31:34.880
are being used as cannon fodder.

00:31:34.880 --> 00:31:41.000
We could stop that war, Biden could stop it next week if he wanted to, but no.

00:31:41.000 --> 00:31:50.280
The military, industrial, congressional, intelligence, media, academia, think tank complex,

00:31:50.280 --> 00:31:56.400
which is what the MIC, Eisenhower spoke of, has turned into, will prevent that.

00:31:56.400 --> 00:32:02.040
And when I say media, right in the middle of all that mickey mat, I mean, the media

00:32:02.040 --> 00:32:05.080
is controlled by the rest of the mickey mat.

00:32:05.080 --> 00:32:10.840
It is the cornerstone and that's why they are beating the drums for war.

00:32:10.840 --> 00:32:17.760
And the naive sophomores that are working for Biden don't seem to realize what nuclear war

00:32:17.760 --> 00:32:19.440
would amount to.

00:32:19.440 --> 00:32:21.040
And that's the real danger here.

00:32:21.040 --> 00:32:28.360
We even see that in the testimony of the top intelligence leaders who say, oh, we don't

00:32:28.360 --> 00:32:31.280
want a nuclear war.

00:32:31.280 --> 00:32:39.440
Putin might resort to nuclear weapons if his backup is up against the wall, conclusion.

00:32:39.440 --> 00:32:42.400
Putin is back up against the wall.

00:32:42.400 --> 00:32:46.920
That policy conclusion is crazy and it makes me very worried.

00:32:46.920 --> 00:32:53.080
Well, we're going to wrap this up, but I want to ask you guys, you're both former

00:32:53.080 --> 00:32:55.320
CIA people.

00:32:55.320 --> 00:33:01.760
And in reality, even when you got a prison for having criticized the torture program as

00:33:01.760 --> 00:33:08.440
John did, you're always going to be CIA people in the eyes of the CIA, meaning that you can't

00:33:08.440 --> 00:33:13.600
write something about anything they've done without getting it cleared at the CIA.

00:33:13.600 --> 00:33:14.600
Correct.

00:33:14.600 --> 00:33:15.600
You're always under.

00:33:15.600 --> 00:33:18.200
So why don't we just talk about that a little bit?

00:33:18.200 --> 00:33:23.400
Because one of the things that I remember as a journalist, I would always run into

00:33:23.400 --> 00:33:30.080
intelligent people from the CIA, and they had everything figured out, except they would

00:33:30.080 --> 00:33:34.400
say, oh, but the president doesn't listen to me or something.

00:33:34.400 --> 00:33:39.520
And what's interesting now is that we aren't having too many people speak up.

00:33:39.520 --> 00:33:45.920
Most of the people you see on television who are former CIA or former military people,

00:33:45.920 --> 00:33:51.040
they're supporting the war, they're supporting things.

00:33:51.040 --> 00:33:55.200
And you guys, how left is these oddballs?

00:33:55.200 --> 00:34:00.600
Why are there so few of you?

00:34:00.600 --> 00:34:05.640
And did you have to get cleared for your remarks on this show today?

00:34:05.640 --> 00:34:07.120
Can you speak your mind?

00:34:07.120 --> 00:34:11.120
How does it work?

00:34:11.120 --> 00:34:14.040
If you don't mind if I start, Ray.

00:34:14.040 --> 00:34:16.480
Sure, go ahead.

00:34:16.600 --> 00:34:25.880
I'm proud to say that I just finished my eighth book, and I submitted it to the CIA for clearance.

00:34:25.880 --> 00:34:32.440
For whatever reason, they sent me an email and said they needed extra time because

00:34:32.440 --> 00:34:35.160
a sister agency wanted to take a look at it.

00:34:35.160 --> 00:34:37.440
I knew that was nonsense.

00:34:37.440 --> 00:34:44.160
So I objected, and I said I didn't sign a secrecy agreement with any sister agency.

00:34:44.240 --> 00:34:48.400
I did what I was supposed to do by submitting it to the CIA.

00:34:48.400 --> 00:34:52.880
And then much to my surprise, they called me on the phone,

00:34:52.880 --> 00:34:55.880
which they never do because they like everything to be in writing,

00:34:55.880 --> 00:34:58.600
so they can use it against you later.

00:34:58.600 --> 00:35:01.680
And they called me and they said, I said, listen,

00:35:01.680 --> 00:35:07.080
I don't care that the FBI thinks they get a chop on my book.

00:35:07.080 --> 00:35:09.720
The FBI doesn't get a chop on my book.

00:35:09.720 --> 00:35:13.720
And the CIA officer told me, well, it's not the FBI.

00:35:13.720 --> 00:35:15.520
It's NSA.

00:35:15.520 --> 00:35:20.880
I said, well, what's NSA care about what I write about surveillance and surveillance detection?

00:35:20.880 --> 00:35:23.000
Well, I don't know, he said.

00:35:23.000 --> 00:35:24.240
We just need two weeks.

00:35:24.240 --> 00:35:26.440
Well, they took five weeks.

00:35:26.440 --> 00:35:32.760
And then when I got the book back, of course, I had missed my publisher's deadline.

00:35:32.760 --> 00:35:36.840
And they had redacted six pages.

00:35:36.840 --> 00:35:41.920
So I wrote back and I said, you redacted six pages and you say that it's classified.

00:35:41.920 --> 00:35:46.360
I want you to know that I got the information off of your website.

00:35:46.360 --> 00:35:50.680
You declassified this information 10 years ago.

00:35:50.680 --> 00:35:52.720
Well, you can appeal.

00:35:52.720 --> 00:35:58.360
You have to print the information from our website and send it back to us.

00:35:58.360 --> 00:36:02.120
And you can appeal it, but we're going to need another 90 days.

00:36:02.120 --> 00:36:06.640
So the publisher, and I said, look, they're doing this on purpose.

00:36:06.640 --> 00:36:07.760
I don't know why.

00:36:07.760 --> 00:36:11.760
I must have angered them somehow.

00:36:11.760 --> 00:36:13.640
And he said, you know what we're going to do?

00:36:13.640 --> 00:36:16.960
We're going to publish it with the pages blacked out.

00:36:16.960 --> 00:36:20.520
And on the cover of the book, we're going to put a starburst.

00:36:20.520 --> 00:36:25.200
And we're going to say, the book, the CIA doesn't want you to read.

00:36:25.200 --> 00:36:28.920
And I said, OK, let's play their own game.

00:36:28.920 --> 00:36:34.920
Now, with that said, I'm pretty good at sending everything that I write

00:36:34.920 --> 00:36:36.720
for clearance to the CIA.

00:36:36.720 --> 00:36:42.520
But there are some things that I think the CIA shouldn't have a vote

00:36:42.520 --> 00:36:44.520
on whether or not I get to publish.

00:36:44.520 --> 00:36:49.400
And so there are some things that I feel strongly about that I don't bother to send.

00:36:49.400 --> 00:36:52.400
And once they sent me a nasty letter,

00:36:52.400 --> 00:36:56.920
and I had my attorney write them a nasty letter back and they dropped it.

00:36:56.920 --> 00:37:03.040
And otherwise, for something like this or an interview that I gave earlier today

00:37:03.040 --> 00:37:05.600
to a Middle Eastern television outlet,

00:37:05.640 --> 00:37:11.560
I do not get cleared because my argument is I don't know what you're going to ask me.

00:37:11.760 --> 00:37:15.400
I don't know in what direction the conversation is going to flow.

00:37:15.600 --> 00:37:18.640
And so there's nothing that I can submit for clearance.

00:37:19.840 --> 00:37:23.480
Well, let me ask a basic question because this is the question

00:37:23.800 --> 00:37:28.080
that is always raised about any totalitarian country.

00:37:28.240 --> 00:37:33.840
Certainly, it's a question Orwell was dealing with in 1984 is

00:37:34.560 --> 00:37:37.480
how many people right now in Russia?

00:37:37.480 --> 00:37:41.560
We know there have to be people who are afraid of Putin's power

00:37:41.760 --> 00:37:43.560
and they hold their silence.

00:37:43.560 --> 00:37:46.360
They don't say what they know and so forth.

00:37:46.360 --> 00:37:50.560
Obviously, we know that we certainly know that's true in communist China.

00:37:50.760 --> 00:37:55.240
There's a top down control power and the Communist Party and so forth.

00:37:55.480 --> 00:38:00.480
But I've always been mystified by the large number of intelligent people

00:38:00.920 --> 00:38:03.600
in a relatively free society.

00:38:03.600 --> 00:38:06.160
We can debate about how free we are.

00:38:06.360 --> 00:38:10.520
Certainly, in wartime, we're not very free and you've gone to jail

00:38:10.720 --> 00:38:12.880
for your views, John Kiriakou.

00:38:13.080 --> 00:38:17.920
But still, I always wonder, you know, how many of your colleagues

00:38:18.120 --> 00:38:25.840
back in the CIA then and now know a different story than the American

00:38:26.040 --> 00:38:30.560
people are told, you know, for example, when we we spent all those years

00:38:30.600 --> 00:38:35.680
fighting international communism and yet anybody with half a brain

00:38:35.880 --> 00:38:39.600
who'd read anything knew that there weren't two communist governments

00:38:39.800 --> 00:38:43.040
in the world that were really getting along well, you know,

00:38:43.240 --> 00:38:46.720
that the Sino-Soviet dispute was a reality and what have you.

00:38:46.920 --> 00:38:51.360
So I would just ask you right now, even though you are the two

00:38:51.560 --> 00:38:55.760
odd men out now, very few people are saying what you're saying.

00:38:56.000 --> 00:39:02.280
And, you know, maybe my career here at NPR will be at an end because I dare

00:39:02.480 --> 00:39:06.160
to have you on, I don't know, I'm wildly exaggerating.

00:39:06.360 --> 00:39:11.840
But nonetheless, how many folks and what scares me about the current moment

00:39:12.040 --> 00:39:17.840
is that there seem to be very few people in the CIA, the Pentagon,

00:39:18.040 --> 00:39:23.520
certainly in the State Department who are questioning.

00:39:23.720 --> 00:39:29.560
That they all seem to be giddy, drunk, really, on a notion of an American

00:39:29.760 --> 00:39:33.560
innocence, that we finally are on the right side.

00:39:33.760 --> 00:39:36.080
This is all about human freedom.

00:39:36.280 --> 00:39:39.240
There's no other way to do it.

00:39:39.440 --> 00:39:45.920
Do you get the feeling that we've had some kind of almost religious conversion?

00:39:46.120 --> 00:39:48.480
Well, Bob, this is Ray McGovern.

00:39:48.680 --> 00:39:51.440
Let me speak to that.

00:39:51.720 --> 00:39:56.760
In all seriousness, I have to confess to being profoundly saddened.

00:39:56.960 --> 00:39:59.760
I would even say scandalized.

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:05.760
my friends, these are my colleagues, some of them I trained in analytic techniques.

00:40:08.240 --> 00:40:14.480
What happened? They must know better. So I don't know how to judge them,

00:40:14.480 --> 00:40:21.600
but I have to come to grips with the reality. People my age should know better. People

00:40:21.600 --> 00:40:28.560
trained analytically should know better. There's a lot of self-censorship. There's a lot of mob

00:40:29.520 --> 00:40:36.560
psychology where our agency couldn't have tortured so many people. What they did for

00:40:36.560 --> 00:40:42.080
God's sake, and it's public knowledge. So in answer to your question, it's really,

00:40:42.080 --> 00:40:48.720
really hard for me to deal with the fact that I don't get to talk to too many of my former colleagues

00:40:48.720 --> 00:40:55.120
because they're afraid that the conversations will be monitored. And this goes way back to

00:40:55.120 --> 00:41:01.040
when I first retired. I'd like to just say a word about my dealings with classified information

00:41:01.040 --> 00:41:07.360
and permission, so to speak, from the agency to publish. I served, as you know, as briefer to

00:41:07.360 --> 00:41:12.880
the President of the United States, and I am very, very careful not to reveal anything of any

00:41:12.880 --> 00:41:17.840
substance having to do with those briefings. That is sacrosanct, and I would never violate

00:41:17.840 --> 00:41:25.760
that trust. Now, I don't write books. I write, I'm a journalist, okay? I learned at the feet of

00:41:25.760 --> 00:41:32.320
Bob Perry after I retired, and I was a current intelligence journalist with access to all kinds of

00:41:33.680 --> 00:41:38.960
sensitive information when I was working at the CIA. So being a journalist, you don't really

00:41:38.960 --> 00:41:44.000
have time to check things out. You just have to be careful not to reveal any secrets that

00:41:44.000 --> 00:41:51.120
really would risk sources and methods. And so that's how I conduct myself. It's okay. They only

00:41:51.120 --> 00:41:57.600
sent me maybe two warning letters a year, and I just ignore them because they don't have a leg to

00:41:57.600 --> 00:42:03.920
stand on. That little contract that I signed that said I won't reveal any information that is

00:42:03.920 --> 00:42:10.400
prejudicial to the national security is just a contract. It's not the Constitution. I took

00:42:10.400 --> 00:42:16.880
a solemn oath to defend and protect the Constitution of the United States against all enemies, foreign

00:42:16.880 --> 00:42:23.760
and domestic. That has a First Amendment in it. That trumps things. When things hit the fan and

00:42:23.760 --> 00:42:31.040
things are serious like what we have now in Ukraine. Again, you don't need to reveal sensitive

00:42:31.040 --> 00:42:37.360
information. One last thing here. When Bill Casey came in under Ronald Reagan, he said at

00:42:37.360 --> 00:42:42.320
his first cabinet meeting, among other things, he said, you know, I can't believe it. I come in

00:42:42.320 --> 00:42:49.280
here and I find out my Soviet analysts, my analysts of the Soviet Union are using 80%

00:42:49.920 --> 00:42:57.360
public sources, newspapers, books to analyze what's going on. They should be using spies.

00:42:57.360 --> 00:43:01.360
Well, give me a break. That's the way it always was. And with the immediate

00:43:02.080 --> 00:43:08.960
the immediate availability of information now, it's 90%. So can the government figure stuff out

00:43:08.960 --> 00:43:16.480
without having satellite photography or intercepted messages poured into his inbox? Normally I can

00:43:16.480 --> 00:43:22.000
and my record speaks for itself. John, you want to get the last word in here?

00:43:22.880 --> 00:43:31.520
